Read-only research task. You must NOT: run any recorder or script, launch any browser/Roam/Obsidian/Chromium, start servers, edit or create files in the repos, or run git stash / reset / checkout / commit. Only read (Read tool, grep, sed -n, wc, `git log`/`git show` are fine).

Context: /mnt/data/projects/dg-demo-videos is a PR-demo-video engine (storyboard-driven Playwright recorder + Kokoro TTS narration + ffmpeg). I'm documenting its architecture as a pipeline diagram. Your scope is the CORE Roam recording pipeline. Another agent covers recordObsidian.ts/recordDuo.ts and another covers README/storyboards, so stay in your lane.

Read these files FULLY (they're long; read every line, in chunks if needed):
- /mnt/data/projects/dg-demo-videos/recorder/recordDemo.ts (~56 KB)
- /mnt/data/projects/dg-demo-videos/recorder/videoTimeline.ts
- /mnt/data/projects/dg-demo-videos/recorder/narrate.py
- /mnt/data/projects/dg-demo-videos/recorder/__tests__/recordDemo.test.ts
- /mnt/data/projects/discourse-graph/apps/roam/scripts/playwright/roamSession.ts, plus any sibling in that directory it imports or that recordDemo imports (e.g. a loadExtension.ts), enough to explain how recordDemo uses it.

Return structured evidence. Every claim gets a file:line ref and, for the important ones, a short verbatim snippet. No recommendations, no opinions. Mark anything you infer rather than read as INFERRED.

1. Entry + CLI: every flag with its default and meaning (quote the header comment verbatim if there is one). Note which flags are ignored/deprecated (e.g. --headless).
2. Storyboard contract as recordDemo consumes it: quote the TypeScript types for storyboard / scene / action. Every action kind and its fields; every scene-level field (caption, toast, pause/hold, assertions, etc.); storyboard-level fields (dist, url, viewport, output, etc.). How the storyboard file is loaded (dynamic import? default export?).
3. The run sequence from main() to the final file, in order. For each stage: the function that implements it, inputs, outputs (files written, temp dirs, naming). Cover specifically:
   a. extension dist validation (what files it requires);
   b. how the Roam session/profile slot is acquired via roamSession (persistent context, profile dir paths, slot locking, "Opening in existing browser session", login wait / waitForRoamReady / #/signin detection, --allow-login), and how the extension is loaded (Roam Depot developer mode? which UI steps?), and --no-roam / --no-extension paths;
   c. window/workspace handling (i3-msg calls, --workspace, floating, window size = viewport, --dpr / deviceScaleFactor);
   d. how video is captured (Playwright recordVideo vs CDP Page.startScreencast vs other; resolution; where the raw file lands);
   e. how a scene runs: action dispatch, the --cursor overlay (arrow, click ripple, glide), toast rendering, caption rendering (in-page overlay or burned later?), js actions and js assertions (how failure is reported), waits/timeouts, "Scene x/y" logging;
   f. timing bookkeeping: how scene start/end timestamps are captured, and how scenes are auto-extended to fit narration length (does narration get generated BEFORE the run so durations are known up front, or after?);
   g. narration: how captions become audio (narrate.py invocation and args, Kokoro model + voice + speed, venv path, per-scene wav files, how durations are measured);
   h. videoTimeline.ts: what problem it solves (clock drift? frame-count drift? head trim? mapping wall-clock to video time?). Quote its exported functions and doc comments;
   i. final composition: EVERY ffmpeg/ffprobe command it builds (trim of setup head, caption burn-in method — subtitle file format or drawtext —, audio placement via adelay/amix/concat, apad, encoder settings like crf/preset, output container), with the exact command-construction lines;
   j. failure path (`-failed.webm`, cleanup, exit codes), and console forwarding (`[recordDemo]` prefix filter).
4. External processes it shells out to (ffmpeg, ffprobe, python, i3-msg, xdotool, dunstctl, etc.) with the exact construction lines.
5. What the unit tests cover (list test names and the functions under test).

Long output is fine; completeness and accuracy matter more than brevity.

